{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1221467-exalted-distilled-homebrew-a-rewrite-wip","id":1221467,"title":"Exalted Distilled: Homebrew, a rewrite (WIP)","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-05-23T07:01:18-07:00","id":1221467,"title":"Exalted Distilled: Homebrew, a rewrite (WIP)","content":"Alright, I lost track of the other thread, but this is a good point to start a new one anyway.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://www.dropbox.com/s/pvsik1zrqy8cpbc/Exalted%20Distilled.zip?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted Distilled</a><br /> <br /> This is now a .zip because the core mechanics and Exalt books have been split up. As well, I'm providing a character sheet that handles character xp calculations for you. (which at the moment is just an excel file. I'll need to figure out how to make an interactive pdf sometime.<br /> <br /> This link will be kept up to date as I work on this project.<br /> <hr />So, Effendoor joined onto the project, and we talked a lot about what to do. He's convinced me to take what I do like about Exalted 3e, and take the good aspects, rather than rewrite the thing from the ground up. So we rewrote the combat engine to use Withering and Decisive damage, but tried to cut all the guff out. (and decoupling Initiative from....Initiative order--and then renamed to Action Points. The word &quot;Initiative&quot; threw me off when trying to learn 3e, they really should have chosen a better word.)<br /> <br /> Actually, &quot;cutting the guff out&quot; was the main focus of this project. It's all about taking what we think works and discarding what we think doesn't to make the system leaner.<br /> <br /> For the project, there are a few <b>target guidelines:</b><ul><li><b>Avoid the &quot;Shadowrun Hacker Problem.&quot; </b> In Shadowrun, for those not familiar, the Hacker subsystem is so convoluted and separate from the rest of mechanics that often GM's will refuse to play both a Hacker with any of the other party members, because either the Hacker gets to do something and the rest of the party doesn't, or the party gets to do things and the Hacker doesn't.<ul><li>Exalted does not get this bad, even in 3e, but my main design goal is that nobody should be twiddling their thumbs during a session of play. Some will <i>shine</i> more than others, of course, but the rules should allow everyone to be engaged.</li> </ul></li> <li><b>Make the game <i>flow</i> during gameplay.</b> This is going to take a lot of playtesting, but one of the main gripes about Exalted 3e that I am aware of is that all of the charms and changes in rules slow down gameplay, particularly combat.<ul><li>Reduce the number and use of &quot;Dice trick&quot; charms.</li> <li>Reduce the number of constant &quot;dice adder&quot; charms.--if it's an effect you are using every round, it should be a permanent enhancement to your character sheet. Otherwise, it should be used when appropriate.<ul><li>Rebalance the mote pools, attack and defense values to compensate.</li> </ul></li> </ul></li> <li><b>Trim down on the number of charms.</b><ul><li>My ultimate design goal for charms is to have a set of 10 general charms, Essence 1-3, that all Exalts can access and color as they wish. Then there will be a bunch of charms that are specific to each Exalt type that showcases their divine nature much more specifically. (typically Essence 3-5, except DB's which are lower.)<ul><li>The &quot;power level&quot; between Exalts is determined by their Essence level. DB's typically stick between Essence 1-3, Celestials 3-4, solars at 5 (due to their Supernal). This allows, for example, a <i>Player Character</i> DB to get to Essence 5 and be as badass as they want, but also keeps the lore intact.</li> <li>Admittedly, part of the reason I have a pool of general charms is practical: it makes it easier for me to balance things out, and cuts down on the work for me.</li> </ul></li> </ul></li> </ul><br /> <br /> <b>Here are a few specific changes I have made. </b>I recommend downloading and reading the document to understand context, though. (I spent a lot of time making it look readable and professional!)<ul><li><b>Sorcerers deal withering damage when gathering motes.</b><ul><li>In a <i>very specific manner</i>, according to the spell. <u> I actually think this is something people should take to the base game as a mod.</u> Sorcerors still kinda checkout during combat.</li> <li>For example: While shaping motes for Cirrus Skiff, the Exalt is whipping winds and clouds around them. So, everyone in Close range has to defend with their Athletics or resistance against these high-speed winds, if not, they lose Action Points (Initiative). Sorcerer's do not gain AP for these withering attacks.</li> <li>It's not a big change, and doesn't do as much damage as an actual attack, but should help Sorcerer's feel like they are actually part of the battle.</li> </ul></li> <li><b>Projects</b><ul><li>Projects are what your character does in your downtime, and replaces the entire Crafting system as well as Sorcerous workings.</li> <li>All characters can access projects: it's how you gather an army, make a spy ring, forge artifacts, etc. etc.</li> <li>It's pretty bare bones right now, but I do plan on expanding it a bit.</li> </ul></li> </ul><br /> <b>Still to do:</b><ul><li><b>Martial Arts</b><ul><li>I'm changing Martial Arts so they are descendants of Abilities, not their own ability.</li> <li>Martial Arts are how I answer the question &quot;How does X characters participate in combat?&quot;<ul><li>Nightingale Style. This will be based solely on your Performance Ability. There should be more of that.</li> <li>There was this completely silly film, &quot;Buddha's Fist&quot; that my friend took me to see. In it, there was a trio of spirits that were strumming <i>very angrily</i> on mandolins (?) and were using that in combat against traditional &quot;martial artists&quot;. That seems like the most Exalted thing I have ever seen.</li> </ul></li> </ul></li> <li><b>Survival and Awareness</b><ul><li>I'm struggling a bit with these two abilities, and got stuck on them. I'm aware they're less fleshed out than the rest.<ul><li>Familiars are cool, but I personally do not like playing with a &quot;pet&quot; in any games. I may have to pull someone who actually <i>likes </i>that mechanic to help me rewrite it.</li> </ul></li> </ul></li> <li><b>More Exalts</b><ul><li>I'm almost done with the Solars, just need to write down their anima abilities (which honestly are probably going to very similar to 3e. I don't really have qualms about that aspect of the rules.</li> <li>Once that is done, I need to move onto making the actual Exalts.</li> </ul></li> </ul><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/845-dragonmystic","name":"Dragonmystic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar845_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"586"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}